description: Git branches and commits as well as testing
globs: 
alwaysApply: false
---
Branch‑Naming Rules

**feature/name

New features: feature/add‑user‑profile

**bugfix/name

Bug fixes: bugfix/fix‑login‑redirect

**hotfix/name

Emergency production fixes: hotfix/patch‑payment‑api

**chore/name

Tooling updates, cleanup: chore/update‑ci‑pipeline

**docs/name

Documentation only: docs/clarify‑api‑usage

Always lowercase, hyphens to separate words, and prefix clearly. Keep it lyrical: every branch tells its tale at a glance.

Commit‑Message Rules

Header line (≤50 chars):

Format: <type>(<scope>): <short description>

Types: feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, perf, test, chore

Example:

scss
Copy
Edit
feat(auth): add JWT refresh‑token endpoint
Blank line

Body (wrap at 72 chars):

Why this change was made, not just what.

Describe any trade‑offs, limitations, or follow‑up work.

Footer (for tracking):

Issue references: Closes #42

Breaking changes:

arduino
Copy
Edit
BREAKING CHANGE: config file renamed from settings.yml to config.yml
Why this old‑school approach endures

Clarity: You see intent at a glance—feature, fix, docs—no guessing.

Traceability: Issue numbers and scopes tie code to conversation.

Consistency: Your team sings from the same hymn‑sheet, and tooling (CI, changelog generators) dances in step.

